# Git & Shell Guardrails

## Git — Never

- Update git config
- Force push to `main`/`master` (warn user if requested)
- `git push --force` unless user explicitly requests it
- Skip hooks (`--no-verify`, `--no-gpg-sign`) unless user explicitly requests
- `git commit --amend` unless all safety conditions are met (user asked, your commit, not pushed)
- Create commits or push unless the user explicitly asks

## Git — Always

- Run `git status`, `git diff`, and `git log` before committing when asked
- Exclude secret files (`.env`, credentials) from commits; warn if user tries to commit them
- Use complete, descriptive commit messages focused on *why*

## Shell — Never

- Destructive commands without explicit user approval (`rm -rf`, `drop database`, etc.)
- Print or log secrets, tokens, or API keys

## Shell — Prefer

- Non-interactive flags (no `-i` on git rebase/add)
- Absolute paths on Windows when paths are ambiguous
